I'm still having trouble assessing if I'm overfitting my model, though. I saw in a previous post that n is sample size, does that mean F2 population? I have 350 of them and 7 chromosomes so I think n/5 might be a bit excessive :) But I also saw that you don't recommend more than 10 parameters and wasn't sure if that was a general rule of thumb or specific to each experimental design. I have been running 10-parameter scans and even with a strict alpha of 0.01 it seems overfitted to me (but I also don't trust my ability to judge these things). For example, the stepwise scan will split a single peak from scanone into 4 separate peaks, two of which are only 0.5cM apart. When I look at my data I only see 9 individuals experiencing a recombination event in the 1.5 lod interval of the single peak from scanone - is that a useful metric to assess if my data supports so much splitting? Do I just always trust the highest pLOD no matter what?

One trait in particular is giving me a really hard time - it is exhibiting 3:1 segregation in my F2s so I got really excited when I saw a single large peak on chr 7 in scanone (plus two tiny peaks *just* over the lod cutoff on chr 2 & 4). The 1.5 lod interval of the large peak spans 11.4Mb and experiences very little recombination. The stepwise approach, on the other hand, selects a model with 5 qtl (step 5 in the attached figure): the familiar ones on chr 2 & 4, a previously undetected peak on chr 6, and the model also splits up the giant peak on chr 7 into two peaks ~10 cM apart that each have drastically lower lods and PVEs than the original peak. However, this model's pLOD is <1 higher than the step 4 model that doesnt split the peak on chr 7, so it only just made it past the penalty.  I don't feel confident choosing the step 5 model over the step 4 model with such a small improvement in pLOD, though I worry that my emotional attachment to a Mendelian locus is clouding my judgement here. Do you have any advice about how to probe this further?

Many thanks for all your help on this!

Molly

